  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Pinpointing French Holidays**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specifics of the 2026 French public holidays. Right now, I\'ve got the date for **Jour de l\'An** - Thursday, January 1st, 2026, which looks like a good opportunity to take a long weekend by taking off Friday, January 2nd!\n\n\n**Analyzing Holiday Advantages**\n\nI am now focusing on maximizing holiday advantages in 2026. Easter, on April 6th, offers a natural 3-day weekend, or potentially 9 days with strategic leave. May presents numerous options: a 3-day Labour Day weekend on May 1st, plus another for Victory Day on May 8th, followed by Ascension on May 14th! By taking off May 15th, I can get another 4-day break.\n\n\n**Maximizing Holiday Time**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on more strategic planning. Pentecost Monday on May 25th provides a standard 3-day weekend. The July 14th holiday is on a Tuesday, presenting a potential four-day break with just one day off on Monday. Armistice Day on November 11th, a Wednesday, yields two solid options for a five-day break with just two leave days! Finally, Christmas offers a 3-day weekend, but potentially 4 with an additional leave day.\n\n\n**Calculating Holiday Gains**\n\nI\'m now refining the bridge day algorithm based on the day of the week. Holidays on Tuesdays yield a four-day weekend with one leave day on Monday. Thursdays also yield a four-day weekend with a Friday leave day. Wednesdays present the best opportunity, with a choice of taking Monday and Tuesday off, or Thursday and Friday, for a five-day break with two leave days. This analysis will guide the calculation.\n\n\n"
